<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-deer">
  <title>When do road accidents with deer happen in Bavaria?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "But like other Bruegels, the painting is in part about details that hide in plain sight. If you didn't know
        the title, you might not notice Icarus at all."
      </p>
      <p>
        --- E. H. Gombrich writing about 'Landscape with the Fall of Icarus' (a painting formerly attributed to Bruegel)
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <em>Background.</em> In some parts of Europe vehicle accidents with deer are a serious problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How many accidents with deer are there? Is it roughly the same across the year? At what
      times of day is the risk greatest?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> <xref ref="hothorn-2020" text="custom">Hothorn (2020)</xref></p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> Two data frames, each of 172596 observations, giving the numbers of vehicle accidents
      every half hour in Bavaria, one for accidents involving deer and one for other accidents.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-deer-accidents">
    <title>Vehicle accidents involving deer</title>

    <p>
      Bavarian data of the numbers of vehicle accidents involving deer over the full ten years from 2002 to 2011
      have been modelled in depth in <xref ref="hothorn-2015" text="custom">Hothorn et al. (2015)</xref>. The data were also used in <xref ref="hothorn-2020" text="custom">Hothorn (2020)</xref> and are available in
      the supplementary material on the publishing journal's website.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-deer1"/> shows a smoothed version of the daily number of vehicle accidents involving
      deer. There was an increase of around 50% over the ten years.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer1">
      <image source="images/deer1-1.png">
        <description>A smooth line plot of the daily number of vehicle accidents involving deer in Bavaria from 2002 to 2011, showing an overall increase of around 50% over the ten years. The y-axis runs from 0 to about 120 accidents per day.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Smoothed numbers of deer-vehicle accidents per day in Bavaria over ten years from Jan 1st 2002 to Dec 31st 2011.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="fig-deer1"/>, and in all the plots in this chapter, the time axis labels are placed at
      the start of the labelled period.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-deer2"/> shows an initial plot to explore monthly seasonality. Each panel of the plot
      displays the aggregate data from that month for each year and the average for the month over the whole time
      series in blue. Here the data are per month, which is why the vertical scale is so different to that in
      <xref ref="fig-deer1"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer2">
      <image source="images/deer2-1.png">
        <description>A monthly subseries plot showing the aggregate deer-vehicle accident counts for each month across the ten years 2002 to 2011, with the overall monthly average shown in blue. Each panel represents one month of the year. There is an overall rise in each monthly series.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Monthly numbers of deer-vehicle accidents across the ten years 2002 to 2011.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There is an overall rise in each monthly series with some, occasionally dramatic, variation, and a possible
      seasonal pattern. Summarising over months is a bit of an approximation, as the months are not all the same
      length. An alternative is to fit another smooth across the data, rougher than the one in
      <xref ref="fig-deer1"/>, and overlay the resulting individual annual series. This has been done in
      <xref ref="fig-deer3"/>. An adaptive smooth has been used.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer3">
      <image source="images/deer3-1.png">
        <description>Annual deer-vehicle accident smooths overlaid from January to December for each of the ten years 2002 to 2011, labelled by year on the right. The lines show a fairly regular pattern with peaks in May-June and October-November and lower values in spring and, to a lesser extent, summer.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Numbers of deer-vehicle accidents across all ten years overlaid from Jan 1st to Dec 31st.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The plot shows a fairly regular pattern across the years with peaks in May-June and October-November and
      lower values in Spring and, to a lesser extent, Summer. The highest numbers are about double the lowest ones.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given the differences in vehicle traffic between weekdays and weekends and public holidays, there could well
      be weekly patterns as well. An initial analysis, without treating public holidays as similar to weekends is
      shown in <xref ref="fig-deer4"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer4">
      <image source="images/deer4-1.png">
        <description>Smoothed day-of-week patterns in the number of deer-vehicle accidents, with separate panels for each day from Sunday to Saturday. Each panel shows the smooth over the ten-year period. Peaks in summer and troughs in winter are visible for all days, with weekends showing a more pronounced seasonal pattern than weekdays.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Day of the week patterns in numbers of deer-vehicle accidents.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A few values of the smoothing parameter were tried and the rounded value leading to this display was chosen,
      as it suggested a clear differentiation between weekdays and weekends. The peaks in summer and the troughs
      in winter stand out. Presumably there is less vehicle traffic at weekends in winter and hence fewer accidents
      with deer then than in summer. The lack of seasonality in the weekday plots offers supporting evidence.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the early days of time series analysis there were suggestions that time series could be decomposed into
      components of trend, seasonality, and noise. Sometimes this works well, sometimes it is difficult to
      disentangle trend and seasonality. That is to some extent the case here.
    </p>

    <p>
      The data available are more detailed than these graphics imply. Numbers of accidents are reported for every
      half-hour of every day, i.e. there are 48 numbers per day. <xref ref="fig-deer6"/> and
      <xref ref="fig-deer7"/> investigate the monthly averages of these daily data across months by year and
      across years by month. Unsurprisingly there are differences in the patterns by years, as the data are
      shifted for the individual months. This is easier to see in <xref ref="fig-deer7"/>, where the two
      peaks are further apart for the summer months and closer together for the winter months. According to
      <xref ref="hothorn-2015" text="custom">Hothorn et al. (2015)</xref> the deer are on the move more around sunrise and after sunset, which explains these peaks
      and their pattern across the year. In that paper, heat maps were used to display the daily data.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-deer6"/> and <xref ref="fig-deer7"/> are a good example of where the choice of
      which time variable to facet by is important. Both plots convey the information, but the second one does
      it better.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer6">
      <image source="images/deer6-1.png">
        <description>Line plots showing average daily patterns of deer-vehicle accidents for each half-hour of the day, faceted by year (ten panels in two rows), with lines coloured by month. Each year shows two daily peaks corresponding to sunrise and sunset activity of deer, with the peaks further apart in summer months and closer together in winter months.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Average daily patterns in numbers of deer-vehicle accidents by month (the coloured lines) and year.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-deer7">
      <image source="images/deer7-1.png">
        <description>Line plots showing daily patterns of deer-vehicle accidents for each half-hour of the day, faceted by month (twelve panels in two rows), with lines coloured by year. The two daily peaks (sunrise and sunset) are further apart in summer months such as June and July, and closer together in winter months such as December and January.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Daily patterns in numbers of deer-vehicle accidents by month across the years (the coloured lines).</p></caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-deer-nondeer">
    <title>What about vehicle accidents without deer?</title>

    <p>
      Data are available over the same period for vehicle accidents not involving deer and these are useful for
      comparison purposes, in particular for studying what features of the deer-vehicle accident data are not
      found in the data for other accidents.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-veh1"/> shows a smoothed version of the daily number of vehicle accidents not
      involving deer, together with the comparable graph for deer-vehicle accidents.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh1">
      <image source="images/veh1-1.png">
        <description>A smooth line plot comparing the daily number of non-deer-vehicle accidents (blue) and deer-vehicle accidents (brown) in Bavaria from 2002 to 2011. Non-deer accidents are at a higher overall level (over twice as many) with little change over time, while deer accidents show the increasing trend seen in the earlier figure.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Smoothed number of non-deer-vehicle accidents (blue) and deer-vehicle accidents (brown) per day in Bavaria over ten years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There is little change over the ten years in non-deer vehicle accidents, with a decline of around 5%. The
      overall level is over twice the level of accidents involving deer. Graphics similar to
      <xref ref="fig-deer2"/> and <xref ref="fig-deer3"/> can be drawn to show that there is a little seasonality
      in the data, but not nearly so much as in the deer-vehicle accident data. A graphic like
      <xref ref="fig-deer4"/> would show that there is not much variation through the week either, apart from
      fewer accidents on a Sunday. The daily pattern of non-deer accidents is shown in
      <xref ref="fig-veh2"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh2">
      <image source="images/veh2-1.png">
        <description>Line plots showing daily patterns of non-deer-vehicle accidents for each half-hour of the day, faceted by month (twelve panels in two rows), with lines coloured by year. Unlike deer accidents, there are higher levels through the day and lower levels in the middle of the night, without the strong dawn and dusk peaks seen in deer accidents.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Daily patterns in numbers of non-deer-vehicle accidents by month across the years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Instead of morning and evening peaks, there are higher levels through the day and lower levels in the
      middle of the night. Interestingly, the vertical scale is the same as that of
      <xref ref="fig-deer7"/>. In fact the half-hours with the highest average rates for deer accidents have
      slightly higher values than the half-hours with the highest average rates for accidents not involving deer,
      despite there being over twice as many non-deer accidents overall.
    </p>

    <p>
      The daily plots have a sawtooth pattern that can also be seen in <xref ref="fig-veh3"/> showing
      just the data for Tuesdays each year.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh3">
      <image source="images/veh3-1.png">
        <description>Line plots showing daily patterns of non-deer-vehicle accidents for Tuesdays only, faceted by year (ten panels in two rows). A sawtooth pattern in the half-hourly counts is visible in each year.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Daily patterns for Tuesdays in numbers of non-deer-vehicle accidents across the years.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Overlaying the Tuesday data for the three years 2008, 2009, 2010 gives <xref ref="fig-veh4"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-veh4">
      <image source="images/veh4-1.png">
        <description>A line plot overlaying the Tuesday non-deer-vehicle accident daily patterns for the years 2008, 2009, and 2010. The three coloured lines show surprisingly close agreement in their sawtooth patterns across all half-hours of the day.</description>
      </image>
      <caption><p>Daily patterns for Tuesdays in numbers of non-deer-vehicle accidents for the years 2008, 2009, 2010.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The agreement across the three years is surprisingly close. Possibly it has something to do with how the
      data were collected and stored.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>Answers.</em> The numbers of vehicle-deer accidents in Bavaria increased over the ten years covered,
      while the numbers of other accidents went down slightly. There are clear seasonal, weekly, and daily patterns
      in vehicle-deer accidents. Drivers should be particularly careful in the months of May, June, November around
      sunrise and sunset.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Further questions.</em> Can reasons for the daily patterns in non-deer vehicle accidents be found?
    </p>

    <subsection>
     <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Axis labels for time series can be placed in different positions for the same data. Whatever is done should be stated. (<xref ref="fig-deer1"/>)</p></li>
        <li><p>Smoothing picks out trends in time series. (<xref ref="fig-deer3"/>)</p></li>
        <li><p>The choice of faceting variable is important. (<xref ref="fig-deer6"/> and <xref ref="fig-deer7"/>)</p></li>
        <li><p>Overlaying time series is effective for checking how similar they are. (<xref ref="fig-veh4"/>)</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
